Full wiring map (MCU ↔ analog board)
Inputs to MCU:

PIN_FIELD_SYNC (MCU pin 2)

Connect to analog divider’s field pulse (~60 Hz or your chosen rate).

Use a resistor (e.g. 10 k) and, if needed, a small transistor buffer if the divider node is delicate.

Outputs from MCU to analog selector network:

Mode select:

PIN_MODE_0 (MCU pin 3) → analog board node MODE_SEL0

PIN_MODE_1 (MCU pin 4) → analog board node MODE_SEL1

These go to transistor selector for frame‑rate divider taps.

Illusion class select:

PIN_CLASS_0 (MCU pin 5) → CLASS_SEL0

PIN_CLASS_1 (MCU pin 6) → CLASS_SEL1

PIN_CLASS_2 (MCU pin 7) → CLASS_SEL2

These go to transistor selector for pulse‑width / phase‑grid networks.

Profile select:

PIN_PROFILE_0 (MCU pin 8) → PROFILE_SEL0

PIN_PROFILE_1 (MCU pin 9) → PROFILE_SEL1

These go to transistor selector for envelope RC networks (Breathing / Drift / Ghostwave / Whiteout).

All MCU outputs should drive transistor bases (via base resistors, e.g. 4.7–10 k) rather than directly switching analog nodes.

Transistor‑level selector schematic (conceptual)
1. Mode selector (frame‑rate taps)
Nodes from divider chain:

FR_30  → 29.97 Hz tap

FR_100 → 100 Hz tap

FR_1000→ 1000 Hz tap

Transistors:

QM1, QM2, QM3 (NPN) as analog switches.

Logic:

MCU pins MODE_SEL0, MODE_SEL1 drive QM1–QM3 bases via resistors.

Only one transistor is turned ON at a time, routing its frame‑rate node to FRAME_CLK_OUT.

Example truth table:

MODE_SEL1 MODE_SEL0

00 → QM1 ON → FRAME_CLK_OUT = FR_30

01 → QM2 ON → FRAME_CLK_OUT = FR_100

10 → QM3 ON → FRAME_CLK_OUT = FR_1000

2. Illusion class selector (pulse‑width / phase network)
Networks:

PW_L0, PW_L1, … PW_L6 → different RC/pulse‑width/phase grids.

Transistors:

QC0–QC6 (NPN) each gating one network to PULSE_CTRL_OUT.

Logic:

MCU pins CLASS_SEL0..2 decoded either:

directly (each transistor base wired to a simple 3‑bit decoder), or

via a small diode/resistor matrix.

Only one QCx ON at a time → selects illusion class.

3. Profile selector (envelope RC networks)
Networks:

ENV_BREATHING

ENV_DRIFT

ENV_GHOSTWAVE

ENV_WHITEOUT

Transistors:

QP0–QP3 (NPN) each connecting one envelope network to LUMA_ENV_IN.

Logic:

MCU pins PROFILE_SEL0..1 decoded to one‑hot control of QP0–QP3.

Board layout plan (CRT Expansion PCB)
Think in three horizontal bands:

Band 1: Timing core (top)
Crystal / master oscillator (Q1 + crystal or RC).

Divider chain (Q2–Q5) generating:

FR_30, FR_100, FR_1000

FIELD_SYNC

PHASE_CLK (virtual horizontal).

Place these close together, short traces, good ground.

Band 2: Illusion envelopes + selectors (middle)
RC networks for:

pulse‑width / phase grids (L0–L6).

envelope profiles (Breathing, Drift, Ghostwave, Whiteout).

Selector transistors:

Mode selector (QM1–QM3).

Class selector (QC0–QC6).

Profile selector (QP0–QP3).

MCU header / footprint:

Place MCU near this band.

Short runs from MCU pins to selector transistor bases.

Band 3: Dot driver + CRT/LED interface (bottom)
Dot driver transistor(s) (QD1, QD2):

Input: PULSE_CTRL_OUT + LUMA_ENV_OUT.

Output:

to CRT video input pad, or

to LED connector pads.

Connectors:

Power header (+5 V / +12 V / GND).

CRT interface header (video in/out, optional sync).

LED header (if you use a physical dot).

Route:

Timing signals downward: FRAME_CLK_OUT, PHASE_CLK, FIELD_SYNC.

Control signals from selectors into dot driver.

Keep:

Analog timing traces short and clean.

MCU traces separated enough to avoid digital noise coupling into the most sensitive analog nodes (crystal, dividers, envelopes).
